1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:02,540
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:05,130
This is the tenth episode of Earthlings 101.

3
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:31,140
Today we will learn about the earthlings favourite
form of communication: language.

4
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:47,890
A language is an acoustical code, produced
by the mouth, and perceived by the ears.

5
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:51,559
Information is encoded in sound groups called
words which are strung together to sequences

6
00:00:51,559 --> 00:00:53,059
called sentences.

7
00:00:53,059 --> 00:00:57,679
A sentence may, for example, transmit the
information that a specific vehicle is infested

8
00:00:57,679 --> 00:01:00,120
by a certain kind of animals.

9
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,659
Language can also be transmitted in visual
form.

10
00:01:02,659 --> 00:01:04,129
This is called writing.

11
00:01:04,129 --> 00:01:07,080
We will speak about writing in another episode.

12
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:10,470
The many languages on earth have their roots
a long time ago.

13
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:15,870
As earthling history is a turmoil of conquest
and migration, languages frequently blend,

14
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:17,190
fusionate and split.

15
00:01:17,190 --> 00:01:20,000
Also, languages are constantly developing.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,730
We will come back to this point later.

17
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:26,820
In order to understand language, we have to
understand how earthlings modelize the world

18
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:28,170
in their mind.

19
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:31,701
Most aliens see the physical world as what
is commonly known as the "conglomerate" - some

20
00:01:31,701 --> 00:01:35,380
kind of four-dimensional energy soup in spacetime.

21
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:37,800
But earthlings don't see reality this way:

22
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,310
First, for earthlings, space and time are
two wholly separate things.

23
00:01:42,310 --> 00:01:46,860
Secondly, their view of the world is all about
matter: Energy at sub-light speed.

24
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:53,690
To describe matter, they have three concepts:
Things, properties, and actions.

25
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:56,300
Things are, so to say, the noodles in the
energy soup.

26
00:01:56,300 --> 00:02:00,350
A thing is a connected bunch of matter, usually
with a distinctive shape.

27
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:02,770
For example, a fruit is a thing.

28
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:06,180
Half a fruit is not a thing, unless you cut
the fruit in two.

29
00:02:06,180 --> 00:02:09,190
Two fruits aren't one thing, unless they are
somehow connected.

30
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:13,370
Then again, a fruit tree is also a thing,
as is a fruit seed.

31
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:16,950
In a larger sense, a thing can be anything
that has a shape and looks like a connected

32
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:20,450
bunch of matter, like the moon, a cloud or
a shadow.

33
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:24,480
According to Sixty Symbols, the "biggest thing
in the universe" is a 4 billion light years

34
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,950
long cluster of 73 quasars.

35
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:30,819
Things are described by thing-words, or nouns.

36
00:02:30,819 --> 00:02:35,560
Now, you can't create a new word for every
thing in the world, so earthlings group similar

37
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:40,220
things into a single category and use the
same word - for example, "star".

38
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:45,040
All multicellular creatures capable of photosynthesis
are "plants", all big plants with wooden stems

39
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:50,420
are "trees", and all mobile creatures that
feed on other creatures are "animals".

40
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:55,970
Except for the microbes.

41
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:59,690
Using the same word for similar things is
not only a trick to simplify language:

42
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:03,349
Grouping things into categories is a basic
feature of the earthling mind.

43
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:07,240
It helps to gain and retain knowledge - for
example, the knowledge that you better run

44
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:10,360
if you see anything that fits into the category
"zombie velociraptor".

45
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:15,420
A second concept is properties : Labels attached
to things by the mind.

46
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:20,590
A property is a common attribute shared by
several things, like "big", or "green".

47
00:03:20,590 --> 00:03:24,150
Properties are, so to say, the taste of the
noodles in the soup.

48
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:27,780
They are specified by another kind of words,
adjectives.

49
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:33,090
Now, things and properties are great to describe
the status quo, a slice of spacetime - but

50
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:36,670
to describe temporal processes, we need something
more.

51
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:39,860
That's where the third concept comes in: Actions.

52
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:45,250
An action is any process that changes either
the position of one or more objects, or their

53
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:46,250
properties.

54
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:50,819
An action is generally thought to originate
from one thing, the "subject" of the action.

55
00:03:50,819 --> 00:03:54,430
Some actions influence other things, the "objects"
of the action.

56
00:03:54,430 --> 00:03:59,670
This concept is closely linked to causality
- a concept I will explain in another episode.

57
00:03:59,670 --> 00:04:03,350
Consider, for example, a fruit falling off
a tree.

58
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:08,840
For most aliens, this is simply a four-dimensional
energy cluster, aligned to a geodesic in the

59
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:09,840
conglomerate.

60
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:13,430
Not so for earthlings: They see this as an
"action" which changes the location of the

61
00:04:13,430 --> 00:04:15,040
fruit in space.

62
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:19,510
Most earthlings would say that the fruit is
the "subject" of the action: The fruit falls.

63
00:04:19,510 --> 00:04:23,820
An earthling physicist might say that the
earth attracts the fruit - thus, the earth

64
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:26,060
is the subject, the fruit is the object.

65
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:29,350
A gardener might say that the tree drops its
fruits.

66
00:04:29,350 --> 00:04:33,400
A religious earthling might believe that it
was the will of god, and a conspiracy theorist

67
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:35,710
may blame the Freemasons.

68
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,880
No matter how they turn it, there is always
an action and a subject, and sometimes an

69
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:41,340
object.

70
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:45,630
Actions are described by action-words, or
verbs, like "fall" or "eat".

71
00:04:45,630 --> 00:04:50,310
Verbs are usually grouped with a word for
the subject, for example "Earthling eats".

72
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:55,240
Additional information is added in form of
other words, for example for objects and properties,

73
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,910
like "The earthling eats the red apple near
the tree".

74
00:04:57,910 --> 00:04:59,520
This is called a sentence.

75
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:03,280
Note that the order of words is important
- the sentence "The red tree eats the earthling

76
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:06,060
near the apple" means something completely
different.

77
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:07,530
Tips for tourists:

78
00:05:07,530 --> 00:05:13,520
When you acquire an universal translator with
English, Mandarin, Spanish, RUssian, French

79
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:16,910
and Arabic, you can get along in most regions
of the world.

80
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:21,300
However, make sure it has an insult filter,
otherwise you may get into trouble.

81
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:26,300
But how do earthlings think and speak about
more non-material matters like space, time,

82
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:29,169
relationships, mathematics, etc.?

83
00:05:29,169 --> 00:05:31,360
That's where abstraction comes in.

84
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,990
For earthlings, there are several levels of
abstraction: The body, the physical world,

85
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:41,940
space, time, more abstract stuff like social
relationships, and pure abstract concepts,

86
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:43,460
like mathematics.

87
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:48,300
The trick is to use concepts of the lower
levels to describe higher levels.

88
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:53,440
For example, the concept of "things" can be
applied to more abstract entities which are

89
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,440
clearly delimited.

90
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:55,970
Also, they reuse words for concrete things
to describe abstract things.

91
00:05:55,970 --> 00:05:57,570
This is called metaphors.

92
00:05:57,570 --> 00:06:05,710
Numbers, for example, are usually described
with vertical spatial metaphors.

93
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:09,500
This comes from the earthling habit of stacking
merchandises vertically.

94
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:15,740
Time is rather described with horizontal spatial
metaphors.

95
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:18,169
This comes from horizontal movement.

96
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:22,669
Common galactic languages are usually developed
by supercomputers, creatures of pure logic

97
00:06:22,669 --> 00:06:25,680
but who had to learn about time, space and
reality.

98
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:30,430
In consequence, those languages have been
developed from the abstract to the concrete.

99
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:35,400
Earthling languages, however, develop the
other way round: From concrete to abstract.

100
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:39,600
Many spatial and temporal expressions come
even from the lowest level of abstraction:

101
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:40,600
The body.

102
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:45,630
An earthling's "back", the "back" of a house,
"moving backwards", "back in the 19th century"

103
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:46,630
etc.

104
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:49,130
Interestingly, some spatial concepts come
from animal bodies.

105
00:06:49,130 --> 00:06:54,570
The "head" of an animal, the "head" of a train",
"looking ahead", "being ahead of one's time"...

106
00:06:54,570 --> 00:06:56,949
Earthlings call this the "zoomorphic model".

107
00:06:56,949 --> 00:07:01,009
But metaphors are also useful to create more
expressive formulations.

108
00:07:01,009 --> 00:07:03,500
Take earthling mating rituals, for example:

109
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:06,390
Instead of saying "I like you, wanna have
sex?",

110
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:11,110
one might say "You are the love of my life,
my sun and moon and stars, the key to my heart,

111
00:07:11,110 --> 00:07:16,010
the sodium to my chlorine and the parmesan
to my spaghetti, wanna have sex?"

112
00:07:16,010 --> 00:07:19,780
Metaphors can't be underrated - actually,
we will see that they are one of the key mechanisms

113
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:22,199
in how language has formed.

114
00:07:22,199 --> 00:07:24,289
Language is in constant development.

115
00:07:24,289 --> 00:07:27,280
Interestingly, earthlings perceive this as
a constant decline.

116
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:30,550
Take, for example, the language called English.

117
00:07:30,550 --> 00:07:34,620
According to many modern earthlings, the English
language is in a sorry state and was much

118
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:36,570
more perfect a century earlier.

119
00:07:36,570 --> 00:07:40,919
But if you travel into the past and ask writers
and savants of past eras, you will hear similar

120
00:07:40,919 --> 00:07:41,919
complaints -

121
00:07:41,919 --> 00:07:44,370
be it from George Orwell in 1946,

122
00:07:44,370 --> 00:07:46,569
Harry Thurston Peck in 1899,

123
00:07:46,569 --> 00:07:48,490
August Schleicher in 1848,

124
00:07:48,490 --> 00:07:50,229
Thomas Sheridan in 1780,

125
00:07:50,229 --> 00:07:52,630
Jonathan Swift in 1712,

126
00:07:52,630 --> 00:07:55,240
or Thomas Sprat in 1667.

127
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:57,780
You'll find the full quotes in the doobly-do.

128
00:07:57,780 --> 00:08:01,730
If there is any consensus amongst linguists
of past centuries, it's probably that the

129
00:08:01,730 --> 00:08:06,780
most perfect language of all time was classical
Latin, two thousand solar cycles ago.

130
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:10,130
Latin scholars of that time, however, would
have begged to differ.

131
00:08:10,130 --> 00:08:15,060
In 46 B.C., for instance, Cicero complained
that the Latin of his time was far less perfect

132
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:16,630
than a century earlier.

133
00:08:16,630 --> 00:08:20,940
What earthlings perceive as decline is actually
a perpetual , cyclic, evolution.

134
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:24,850
It's like the perceived downfall of a spacecraft
which is actually an orbit.

135
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:29,210
This perpetual evolution of language can be
compared to the Heteromorphs of Galarius.

136
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:33,260
Those polymorphic creatures live in the high
atmospheric pressure of the gas giant Galarius

137
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:36,510
Seven and are coordinated by a global telepathic
field.

138
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:41,140
One could compare single creatures to words,
body parts to syllables, and groups of creatures

139
00:08:41,140 --> 00:08:43,380
to expressions and sentences.

140
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:47,230
The constant change of the Heteromorphs is
based on four mechanisms:

141
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:50,350
Fusion, Erosion, Assimilation and Combination.

142
00:08:50,350 --> 00:08:54,351
Fusion means that due to the high atmospheric
pressure, groups of creatures are fused, so

143
00:08:54,351 --> 00:08:58,410
several individuals become mere organs of
one single organism.

144
00:08:58,410 --> 00:09:02,970
Erosion means that body parts are constantly
eroded by violent storms.

145
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:06,140
Assimilation means that the telepathic field
of the population makes creatures of the same

146
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:10,660
kind more and more similar, such that they
get identical members and organs.

147
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:14,500
But through the process of erosion and assimilation,
creatures become weaker.

148
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:19,360
Hence the fourth mechanism: Combination, the
capacity to form groups which are able to

149
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,430
do wholly new tasks.

150
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:23,980
It's easy to see that this forms an endless
loop:

151
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:28,470
Creatures form groups, fuse together, are
assimilated and eroded, become smaller and

152
00:09:28,470 --> 00:09:30,950
weaker, combine again etc.

153
00:09:30,950 --> 00:09:34,240
Now, Earthling languages follow a similar
cycle:

154
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:38,090
Fusion, Erosion, Assimilation, and Metaphors.

155
00:09:38,090 --> 00:09:42,330
Fusion means that groups of words tend to
fuse together, often forming suffixes and

156
00:09:42,330 --> 00:09:43,700
prefixes.

157
00:09:43,700 --> 00:09:47,760
This creates what earthlings call conjugations
and declinations.

158
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:50,890
Erosion means that words are shortened and
sounds shift such that they are easier to

159
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:52,870
pronounce.

160
00:09:52,870 --> 00:09:56,110
Assimilation means that differences between
words tend to be smoothed.

161
00:09:56,110 --> 00:10:00,180
For example, conjugations of verbs become
more and more similar.

162
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:02,950
This is how general grammar rules form.

163
00:10:02,950 --> 00:10:05,839
Those three mechanisms come from earthlings'
laziness:

164
00:10:05,839 --> 00:10:09,450
They make language simpler to pronounce and
rules easier to memorize.

165
00:10:09,450 --> 00:10:12,630
But they also reduce the expressiveness of
words.

166
00:10:12,630 --> 00:10:15,930
That's where the fourth mechanism comes into
play: Metaphors.

167
00:10:15,930 --> 00:10:21,510
As mentioned before, metaphors mean that words,
or groups of words, are used to express something

168
00:10:21,510 --> 00:10:23,019
completely new.

169
00:10:23,019 --> 00:10:26,700
This is the main constructive mechanism of
language.

170
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:30,940
Like the life cycle of heteromorphs, the evolution
of language is an endless cycle of creation

171
00:10:30,940 --> 00:10:32,370
and destruction:

172
00:10:32,370 --> 00:10:37,050
Words are combined to new, colorful metaphors,
they fuse, are eroded and assimilated until

173
00:10:37,050 --> 00:10:39,050
they are mutilated and worn-out.

174
00:10:39,050 --> 00:10:42,930
Eventually they are combined to new expressive
metaphors, and so on.

175
00:10:42,930 --> 00:10:47,500
The whole building of language is nothing
else than an asteroid reef of dead metaphors.

176
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,930
In other words, the etymology of language
is inherently fractal:

177
00:10:50,930 --> 00:10:55,300
Words are old groups of words, which were
old groups of words, which were old groups

178
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:59,380
of words, and so on.

179
00:10:59,380 --> 00:11:01,860
Strategic advice.

180
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:03,790
Earthlings' language can be misleading.

181
00:11:03,790 --> 00:11:07,770
For example, the frequent use of the word
"tanks" in radio messages may lead you to

182
00:11:07,770 --> 00:11:11,500
the conclusion that the earthlings will use
large tank vehicles to spray your troops with

183
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:13,160
deadly hydric acid.

184
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:17,839
However, once you have equipped all your soldiers
with protective gear against hydric acid,

185
00:11:17,839 --> 00:11:22,209
you may discover that military tanks are actually
something completely different.

186
00:11:22,209 --> 00:11:26,290
To demonstrate the evolution of language,
let's look at the english word "gonna".

187
00:11:26,290 --> 00:11:30,160
In modern English, this word indicates near
future: "We're gonna eat."

188
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:35,020
It's an abbreviation of "going to", but only
in the sense "about to", not "walking to".

189
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:39,140
Hundreds of solar cycles ago, "going to" was
only used in the context "going or traveling

190
00:11:39,140 --> 00:11:40,290
to a place".

191
00:11:40,290 --> 00:11:45,090
Then, in what earthlings call the 15th century,
"going to do something" appeared, but only

192
00:11:45,090 --> 00:11:49,230
in the sense "going somewhere, in order to
do something", for example soldiers who are

193
00:11:49,230 --> 00:11:51,089
going to bring a prisoner to a castle.

194
00:11:51,089 --> 00:11:54,800
From there, the meaning shifted gradually
towards a more abstract meaning,

195
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:58,480
and in the seventeenth century, we read of
an arms depot which is "going to be taken

196
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:01,130
away" - the arms depot is certainly not walking
anywhere.

197
00:12:01,130 --> 00:12:07,580
So, "going to" has become, as a grammar manual
from 1646 puts it, "a sign of the future".

198
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:10,660
From there, the future "going to" became more
and more popular.

199
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:15,250
Then, at the beginning of the twentieth century,
an eroded variant of "going to" appears:

200
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:18,560
The word "gonna", as it is used till this
day.

201
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:20,040
Scientific advice:

202
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:26,450
Many earthlings believe that language and
logic are uniquely processed by the left half

203
00:12:26,450 --> 00:12:31,019
of the brain, whereas things like creativity,
images and music are solely processed by the

204
00:12:31,019 --> 00:12:32,019
right half.

205
00:12:32,019 --> 00:12:33,070
That is utter nonsense.

206
00:12:33,070 --> 00:12:40,403
So, if you swap the left halves of the brains
of a french-speaking painter and an english-speaking

207
00:12:40,403 --> 00:12:41,403
musician,

208
00:12:41,403 --> 00:12:42,670
you don't get a french-speaking musician and
a english-speaking painter -

209
00:12:42,670 --> 00:12:46,230
you just get two very messed up earthlings.

210
00:12:46,230 --> 00:12:49,740
But now, let's travel into the future of the
English language.

211
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:51,640
Consider the verb "To have".

212
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:56,481
Traditionally, this is conjugated "I have,
you have, he has, we have, you have, they

213
00:12:56,481 --> 00:12:57,481
have".

214
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:00,630
In the present, erosion has created the shorter
forms:

215
00:13:00,630 --> 00:13:04,839
I've, You've, He's, We've, You've, They've.

216
00:13:04,839 --> 00:13:08,649
The pronoun and the verb are about to fuse
into one single word.

217
00:13:08,649 --> 00:13:13,100
In some regions, the plural "You've", has
been replaced by "Y'all've", a short form

218
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:14,570
of "You all have".

219
00:13:14,570 --> 00:13:18,029
In a hundred solar cycles, the apostrophe
will be dropped and the pronouns will have

220
00:13:18,029 --> 00:13:23,600
become prefixes: Ive, Youve, Hes, Weve, Yallve,
Theyve.

221
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:27,730
Some centuries later, the conjugation will
be simplified through assimilation to Ive,

222
00:13:27,730 --> 00:13:31,420
Youve, Heve, Weve, Yallve, Theyve.

223
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:35,910
Eventually, erosion will replace "Ive", by
the easier to pronounce "Eve", and "Yallve",

224
00:13:35,910 --> 00:13:36,910
by "Yave".

225
00:13:36,910 --> 00:13:41,620
Eight hundred solar cycles from now, all english
verbs will have adapted the same prefixes

226
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:44,260
for present tense - again, through assimilation:

227
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:48,510
Eetalk, Youtalk, Hetalk, Wetalk, Yatalk, Theetalk.

228
00:13:48,510 --> 00:13:52,750
The future tense will be marked by an eroded
"gonna":

229
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:58,579
Eenatalk, Younatalk, Henatalk, Wenatalk, Yanatalk,
Theenatalk,

230
00:13:58,579 --> 00:14:01,480
and the past tense a crippled, "have":

231
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:06,000
Eevetalk, Youvetalk, Hevetalk, Wevetalk, Yavetalk,
Theevetalk.

232
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,440
But in the same time, the mecanics of metaphor
will build new colorful expressions.

233
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:14,779
For example, a thousand years for now, the
invention of time travel will require a whole

234
00:14:14,779 --> 00:14:16,940
new set of grammatical tenses.

235
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:21,500
For instance, to say that someone did a time
jump to escape death, one would say "Heve

236
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:24,060
jum fro’die", "He has jumped from dying".

237
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:29,050
So, the verb "jump", originally a body movement,
becomes an auxiliary verb for time-travel

238
00:14:29,050 --> 00:14:31,050
tenses.

239
00:14:31,050 --> 00:14:34,860
By the way, this episode is partially based
upon the earthling book "The Unfolding of

240
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:35,959
Language".

241
00:14:35,959 --> 00:14:38,880
It describes in great detail how language
has developed.

242
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:41,220
